    <body>
        <segment id="1" parent="65" relname="span">My views about what HCI aspects are necessary for good Web design:</segment>
        <segment id="2" parent="69" relname="span">Making a conclusion about whether a website is designed well is relative to the purpose of the design.</segment>
        <segment id="3" parent="70" relname="span">I believe a webs tie can be classified under one of the three purpose-driven categories, informative, interactive, or both informative and interactive.</segment>
        <segment id="4" parent="5" relname="condition">If the website is created as an introduction to new consumers,</segment>
        <segment id="5" parent="81" relname="span">then aspects such as user-centeredness, and attractiveness, are not as crucial for the designers.</segment>
        <segment id="6" parent="74" relname="concession">The designers should keep these ideas in the back of their minds,</segment>
        <segment id="7" parent="74" relname="span">however, aspects such as accuracy, legibility, and updatedness, are more crucial.</segment>
        <segment id="8" parent="78" relname="span">For example, Walgreens.com about four years ago had a bland website.</segment>
        <segment id="9" parent="8" relname="elaboration">It consisted of historical background information, a store locator, and other miscellaneous features.</segment>
        <segment id="10" parent="78" relname="elaboration">It did not bear any of the interactive features, which is currently does, such as an online pharmacy feature, online store, the ability to upload photos from your digital camera, etc.</segment>
        <segment id="11" parent="79" relname="span">It has come a long way.</segment>
        <segment id="12" parent="11" relname="background">However, still features the informative content.</segment>
        <segment id="13" parent="79" relname="nonvolitional-cause">The cause of this transformation, could be that online customers have come to except this type of service from a large retailer, like Walgreens.</segment>
        <segment id="14" parent="76" relname="summary">One may conclude that the purpose of the website has changed from mostly informative to both informative and interactive.</segment>
        <segment id="15" parent="83" relname="volitional-cause">On the other hand, when the purpose of a website is more centered towards mandated interaction such as the case with an e-commerce website,</segment>
        <segment id="16" parent="83" relname="span">most (if not all) HCI concepts are pertinent.</segment>
        <segment id="17" parent="84" relname="span">Concepts such as the following:</segment>
        <segment id="18" parent="85" relname="list">Attractiveness: An e-commerce website must be ?attractive? to its target market.</segment>
        <segment id="19" parent="85" relname="list">Awareness: This site must make itself noticeable and make consumers ?aware? of its services and products.</segment>
        <segment id="20" parent="87" relname="span">User friendliness and centered: The site should have a customized option whether the site recognizes frequent users.</segment>
        <segment id="21" parent="20" relname="volitional-result">Thereby, giving the site more ?user centered?.</segment>
        <segment id="22" parent="89" relname="span">The site should seem feasible in usage.</segment>
        <segment id="23" parent="24" relname="volitional-cause">If the consumer is confused by the layout or too much graphics and information,</segment>
        <segment id="24" parent="88" relname="span">then the website is deemed ?unfriendly to the user?.</segment>
        <segment id="25" parent="66" relname="span">In my design course for my undergrad, one of the more emphasized points was to compare and contrast what is out there.</segment>
        <segment id="26" parent="25" relname="elaboration">The designers? should see what works.</segment>
        <segment id="27" parent="68" relname="span">Moreover they should check off the below, which are user experience goals from Preece p.18:</segment>
        <segment id="28" parent="67" relname="list">1. Satisfying</segment>
        <segment id="29" parent="67" relname="list">2. Enjoyable</segment>
        <segment id="30" parent="67" relname="list">3. Fun</segment>
        <segment id="31" parent="67" relname="list">4. Entertaining</segment>
        <segment id="32" parent="67" relname="list">5. Helpful</segment>
        <segment id="33" parent="67" relname="list">6. Motivating</segment>
        <segment id="34" parent="67" relname="list">7. Aesthetically pleasing</segment>
        <segment id="35" parent="67" relname="list">8. Supportive of creativity</segment>
        <segment id="36" parent="67" relname="list">9. Rewarding</segment>
        <segment id="37" parent="67" relname="list">10. Emotionally fulfilling</segment>
        <segment id="38" parent="59" relname="preparation">What websites experts say:</segment>
        <segment id="39" parent="59" relname="span">The design experts at Designtech.com recommend a five-step approach to evaluate the website usability.</segment>
        <segment id="40" parent="60" relname="list">The first step is to ?Identify the purpose, goals, and objectives of the website, clarify issues, questions, and concerns.?</segment>
        <segment id="41" parent="61" relname="span">The second approach is to ?Characterize classes of users and develop user-personas.?</segment>
        <segment id="42" parent="62" relname="span">This step is so crucial to good website design.</segment>
        <segment id="43" parent="64" relname="antithesis">For example, a toy company for war-oriented action figures should not use pastel background and classical music on its website.</segment>
        <segment id="44" parent="45" relname="condition">If the understand their audience,</segment>
        <segment id="45" parent="64" relname="span">they should have camouflage-type background and aggressive music.</segment>
        <segment id="46" parent="54" relname="preparation">My points:</segment>
        <segment id="47" parent="56" relname="span">I believe with time these will be phased out.</segment>
        <segment id="48" parent="47" relname="volitional-cause">The well-designed sites will outrun the poorly designed ones.</segment>
        <segment id="49" parent="56" relname="restatement">In other words, the theory of the ?survival of the fittest? may avail.</segment>
        <segment id="50" parent="51" relname="volitional-cause">The online users are becoming more comfortable conducted transactions of the web,</segment>
        <segment id="51" parent="57" relname="span">therefore, the business must make their website more user-centered, in order to be successful.</segment>
